Flow Fairness Evaluation and Comparison

1. Introduction

Flow fairness refers to how equally available network resources, particularly bandwidth, are distributed among competing traffic flows.

In bandwidth-constrained networks, multiple flows may compete for limited transmission capacity. Different packet scheduling algorithms manage these competing demands differently.

This research will evaluate the fairness of First-In-First-Out (FIFO), Priority Queuing (PQ), and Weighted Fair Queuing (WFQ) using Jain's Fairness Index.

2. Understanding Flow Fairness

A scheduling algorithm is considered fair when competing flows receive an appropriate and reasonably equitable share of available network resources.

However, fairness does not necessarily mean that every flow must receive exactly the same bandwidth.

For example, Weighted Fair Queuing intentionally allocates bandwidth according to assigned weights. Therefore, fairness must be interpreted in relation to the scheduling policy and configured weights.

3. Jain's Fairness Index

The study will use Jain's Fairness Index to measure how equally throughput is distributed among competing flows.

Formula:

J = (x1 + x2 + ... + xn)² / [n(x1² + x2² + ... + xn²)]

Where:

- J represents Jain's Fairness Index.
- x represents the throughput received by an individual flow.
- n represents the number of competing flows.

The index ranges from 1/n to 1 when all measured throughputs are positive.

A value closer to 1 indicates more equal throughput distribution.

4. Example Fairness Calculation

Consider four competing network flows with the following measured throughputs:

Flow| Throughput
Flow 1| 5 Mbps
Flow 2| 5 Mbps
Flow 3| 5 Mbps
Flow 4| 5 Mbps

Using Jain's Fairness Index:

J = (5 + 5 + 5 + 5)² / [4(5² + 5² + 5² + 5²)]

J = 400 / 400

J = 1.00

This indicates perfectly equal throughput distribution.

If one flow receives substantially more bandwidth than the others, the fairness index will decrease.

5. Fairness Evaluation of Scheduling Algorithms

5.1 FIFO

FIFO transmits packets in their order of arrival.

Although it treats packets according to arrival order, it does not explicitly allocate bandwidth equally among competing flows.

Consequently, a high-volume flow may occupy a large proportion of the available queue capacity and transmission opportunities.

5.2 Priority Queuing (PQ)

PQ classifies traffic into different priority levels.

Higher-priority queues receive preferential service over lower-priority queues.

This can result in unequal bandwidth allocation and possible starvation of lower-priority flows when high-priority traffic continuously arrives.

5.3 Weighted Fair Queuing (WFQ)

WFQ allocates service among competing flows according to assigned weights.

Its design aims to provide controlled bandwidth sharing while allowing different flows to receive different service allocations.

The fairness evaluation must consider whether the observed throughput distribution corresponds to the configured weights.

